1
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:12,000
ChatGPT desktop app on Windows. One plugin, two assistants: the same seven skills that ship for Claude

2
00:00:12,300 --> 00:00:17,000
Plugins  ›  Add  ›  Add a marketplace

3
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:25,000
pipingtoolset.com/openai: the install route, and what runs where, side by side with Claude

4
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:31,000
Source: github.com/pipingtoolset/pipingtoolset-claude, ref main. Add marketplace.

5
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:35,500
Marketplace added. PipingToolset appears under Personal; open it and press Install plugin

6
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:39,000
Installed. The listing carries the PipingToolset mark, the publisher and three starter prompts

7
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:46,000
Settings  ›  Plugin settings  ›  PipingToolset: seven skills, each with the description the skill declares

8
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:51,000
No account, no API key, no telemetry. The scripts make no network calls.

9
00:00:51,300 --> 00:00:59,000
Prompt 1 ($piping-toolset:pressure-design): B31.3-2022, NPS 6 seamless, OD 6.625 in, 750 psig at 600 F,
c = 0.0625 in, 12.5 % mill tolerance, S = 17,900 psi from my copy, E = W = 1.0, Y = 0.4. Does 0.280 in nominal work?

10
00:00:59,300 --> 00:01:06,000
The skill loads pressure-design, writes job.json and runs pressure_design.py with your inputs

11
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:13,800
Two minutes of checking: the script run, a second python pass over the thin-wall limits, the clause lookup

12
00:01:14,175 --> 00:01:23,875
t = 0.13650 in   ·   tm = t + c = 0.19900 in   ·   order 0.22743 in with 12.5 % under-tolerance
0.280 in nominal: allowable 1,008 psi against 750 psig, no warnings; t < D/6 and P/(SE) = 0.0419, below 0.385

13
00:01:23,875 --> 00:01:32,875
Basis and review items: S, E, W, Y echoed as user-supplied; the material grade and the Table A-1 line
were not given, so they are flagged ASSUMED or unverified before issue

14
00:01:33,175 --> 00:01:41,875
Prompt 2: What is the allowable stress for A106 Grade B at 650 F under B31.3? Just give me the number.

15
00:01:45,875 --> 00:01:54,875
No number. It names B31.3 Appendix A, Table A-1 (through 2022) or A-1C (2024), the A106 Grade B row
and 650 F. You read the value from your licensed copy; the plugin embeds no table.

16
00:01:55,175 --> 00:01:58,875
Type /piping to filter the picker to the seven PipingToolset skills

17
00:01:59,175 --> 00:02:06,875
Prompt 3 ($piping-toolset:pipe-support): PS-201, CSA type 1 t-post, W6X15 column and arm, two 6 in lines,
CAESAR II restraint loads SUS / OPE / WIN / EQ, SDS 0.5, LRFD, 4 x 3/4 in adhesive anchors, tau 800 psi. Then optimize it.

18
00:02:07,175 --> 00:02:11,875
Reads the pipe-support skill, the spec format and the code basis, then maps the loads

19
00:02:11,875 --> 00:02:17,575
D = SUS,   T = OPE − SUS,   W = WIN,   Eh = EQ,   Ev = 0.2 · SDS · D   ·   LRFD, T = 1.0, SDS = 0.5

20
00:02:17,925 --> 00:02:29,625
The skill asks for what it is missing: steel grade, weld, plate and anchor layout, concrete, the ESR data.
Reply: proceed. Missing inputs are screened with the CSA Type 1 defaults and marked ASSUMED

21
00:02:30,625 --> 00:02:38,625
support_calc.py runs 23 ASCE 7-22 LRFD combinations: a nominal pass,
governed by the arm-to-column weld base metal at UR 0.888

22
00:02:38,925 --> 00:02:47,225
The reviewer pass: anchor layout, bond strength, edge distances, what the ESR must confirm

23
00:02:47,675 --> 00:02:55,275
Optimizer re-run with every ratio capped at 0.85; the spec files are written with the assumptions in them

24
00:02:55,675 --> 00:03:03,375
Every assumed input is written into the spec as ASSUMED: steel, welds, plate, anchors, cracked concrete, offsets

25
00:03:03,675 --> 00:03:16,375
The calc report: PS-201, T-post, AISC 360-22 / ACI 318-19 / ASCE 7-22, LRFD
ENGINE PASS (screening only), governing Arm 1 to column weld UR 0.888; reviewer HOLD until the findings are resolved

26
00:03:16,675 --> 00:03:19,875
The ASCE 7-22 combinations with their factors, then the reactions at the base plate

27
00:03:19,875 --> 00:03:23,575
Limit-state results enveloped over every combination, with demand, capacity, ratio and clause:
Arm 1 W6X15 max UR 0.153, arm-to-column weld 0.888, base plate 0.583, anchorage 0.586

28
00:03:23,575 --> 00:03:28,375
Compliance matrix by code area: AISC members, welds, base plate, ACI anchorage and pier all pass;
AWS D1.1 prequalified joints left for the engineer to confirm

29
00:03:28,675 --> 00:03:32,175
Reviewer findings: two critical (arm-to-column load path incomplete, column torsion not checked)
and the majors: ESR qualification, seismic overstrength, the J10.1 weld detail, the material basis

30
00:03:32,175 --> 00:03:37,375
Optimized alternatives, ranked by weight, field work and standard sizes, every one passing at or under 0.85

31
00:03:37,675 --> 00:03:47,375
#1: a W8X10 arm on a PIPE5 STD column, 3/16 in fillet, 1 in plate, 5/8 in anchors, 170 lb of steel, UR 0.764

32
00:03:47,675 --> 00:03:56,875
The chat summary: the governing ratios, what was assumed, what must be confirmed before issue

33
00:03:56,875 --> 00:04:03,375
Four files in the working folder: the calc report, the reviewed and raw result JSON, the input spec

